## Category C: Product Economics (From Cost to Business Value)

**11. Show "cost per user served" for each data product.**
Line 261-296: product economics shows costs. But cost without consumption context is meaningless. A $500/month data product serving 50 users costs $10/user/month. One serving 2 users costs $250/user/month. The latter is the one to question.

*Reasoning:* Absolute cost doesn't justify elimination. Cost per consumer does. A $2,000/month product serving the entire executive team is cheap per capita. A $200/month product nobody reads is waste.

**12. Add "data product SLA" — does this product meet its freshness/availability contract?**
Products have cost, but no service level. If a "Daily Revenue Report" product has a $300/month cost but delivered late 4 times this month, its cost/quality ratio is terrible. Cross-reference product cost against pipeline SLA data.

*Reasoning:* A cheap product that's unreliable wastes the cost of everyone who waits for it or works around it. Quality × cost = total cost of ownership.

**13. Show "product trend" — is this product's cost growing or shrinking?**
Month-over-month cost trend per product. A product whose cost doubled in 3 months needs attention regardless of its absolute level. One that's stable at a high level may be correctly sized.

*Reasoning:* Same as portfolio trend reasoning — static views miss trajectory. Product owners need to know whether their product is becoming more expensive over time.

**14. Add "product retirement candidates" — products with declining usage AND high cost.**
Cross-reference product cost against access history (last N days of reads). A product with $400/month cost but only 2 queries in 30 days is a retirement candidate. Flag it.

*Reasoning:* Nobody actively decides to keep paying for unused data products. They just don't decide to stop. Surfacing the question forces the decision.

**15. Link products to their upstream pipelines and downstream consumers.**
A data product exists in a graph: pipelines feed it, consumers read it. Show both. "This product is fed by pipeline X (cost: $100/month) and consumed by 3 dashboards and 12 ad-hoc users."

*Reasoning:* Product economics viewed in isolation miss the full cost (including the pipeline that feeds them) and the full value (including all consumers, not just direct queries).

