## Category F: Experiments (From Planning to Verification)

**24. Show experiment DURATION alongside status.**
Lines 485-545: experiments table shows status. But there's no "how long has this experiment been running?" An experiment in RUNNING status for 45 days probably needs to be forced to a decision. Show: "RUNNING (38 days)" to create completion pressure.

*Reasoning:* Without duration, experiments linger. "RUNNING" for 3 days is fine. "RUNNING" for 2 months is a decision someone is avoiding. Duration makes the avoidance visible.

**25. Add "experiment health" — is the metric actually changing during the experiment?**
An experiment on auto-suspend expects idle credits to decrease. But is that ACTUALLY happening during the experiment window? Show a before/after trend of the target metric. "Expected: idle credits ↓. Observed: idle credits ↓23%. Experiment appears effective."

*Reasoning:* An experiment without intermediate measurement is just a wait-and-see. Early measurement lets operators terminate failed experiments early (saving time) or confirm successful ones fast.

**26. Auto-suggest experiment VERIFICATION when the observation window expires.**
If an experiment was created with an expected duration (implicitly: the "observation" status exists for this reason), auto-prompt when enough time passes: "Experiment X has been observing for 14 days. Ready to verify? [Verify now]."

*Reasoning:* Experiments that don't get verified become stale data. A nudge at the right time completes the loop.

**27. Link experiments to their source ACTION — and show the action's current state.**
V081 unified experiment-verify with action-close. But the UI should make this visible: "This experiment came from action #47 (Tighten auto-suspend on WH_ALFA_QUERY). Action status: IN_PROGRESS. On verification, the action will auto-close."

*Reasoning:* The experiment exists because of an action. Showing the connection helps the operator understand the full workflow state without navigating between pages.

**28. Show "experiment portfolio" statistics — how many experiments are in each status?**
A KPI row at the top of the Experiments section: "Planned: 3 | Running: 2 | Observing: 1 | Verified: 8 | Rejected: 2 | Rolled back: 1." This is the meta-view of optimization throughput.

*Reasoning:* Without aggregate statistics, the operator doesn't know if the experiment pipeline is flowing or stuck. Low "Verified" count relative to "Running" means decisions aren't being made.

