## Category I: User Experience & Usability

**36. The 6-section layout ("Portfolio, SLOs, Products, Cost Truth, Scenarios, Experiments") needs a visual flow indicator.**
These sections represent a workflow: Understand (Portfolio, Cost Truth) → Plan (Products, Scenarios) → Execute (Experiments) → Verify (back to Portfolio). But the section nav is flat pills with no flow indication. Add a numbered sequence or progress indicator.

*Reasoning:* A workflow without visual sequence looks like 6 random panels. A numbered flow says "start here, end there" and teaches the user the intended path.

**37. Add a "planning summary" that synthesizes across all 6 sections into one view.**
After visiting all sections, the user has to mentally combine: portfolio priorities + SLO status + product economics + cost truth + scenario projections + experiment status. Provide a one-screen "Decision Summary" that pulls the headline KPI from each section.

*Reasoning:* A director reviewing the Decision Studio for a weekly planning meeting wants: "Here's where we are across all dimensions" in one glance. Currently they must click through 6 sections to assemble that picture.

**38. Add "saved scenarios" — let users name and compare scenario configurations.**
`scenario_projection` takes adoption% and realization% as inputs. But there's no way to save: "Aggressive (80% adoption, 60% realization) vs Conservative (40% adoption, 40% realization)" and compare them side by side.

*Reasoning:* Planning involves comparing alternatives. Without saved scenarios, the user has to remember slider positions and mentally compare. Saving them enables rigorous comparison.

**39. The experiment "Save" button should show a PREVIEW of what will happen.**
Line 541: "Save experiment" executes immediately. But the user should see: "Saving this as VERIFIED will: (1) book $400 to SAVINGS_LEDGER, (2) close ACTION #47, (3) mark experiment terminal." Make the cascade visible before execution.

*Reasoning:* V081 unified experiment-verify into an atomic transaction. The user should understand all the side effects of that transaction BEFORE clicking. Surprises after the click erode trust.

**40. Add "Decision Studio ROI dashboard" — track the studio's own contribution to savings.**
Across the entire page, show one persistent KPI: "Decision Studio recommendations verified this quarter: $X. Actions created: N. Average time-to-verify: Y days." This is the page's own performance metric.

*Reasoning:* A planning tool that can't demonstrate its own value will eventually be deprioritized. Self-measurement is self-justification. If the Decision Studio contributes $20,000/quarter in verified savings, it earns its existence.

